{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fnil System;}}
{\colortbl\red0\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green255\blue255;\red0\green255\blue0;\red255\green0\blue255;\red255\green0\blue0;\red255\green255\blue0;\red255\green255\blue255;\red0\green0\blue127;\red0\green127\blue127;\red0\green127\blue0;\red127\green0\blue127;\red127\green0\blue0;\red127\green127\blue0;\red127\green127\blue127;\red192\green192\blue192;}
{\info{\creatim\yr2005\mo7\dy7\hr17\min32\sec55}{\revtim\yr2005\mo7\dy7\hr17\min32\sec56}{\version1}{\vern262245}}\paperw12240\paperh15840\margl14\margr60517\margt14\margb64117\deftab720

#{\footnote Hlp_Description}
${\footnote Description}
K{\footnote Description}
+{\footnote Browse:0005}
\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Description}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Program: Energy Explorer (Version 2005-05-15).}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Developer: Kelly Kissock}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Operating Systems: Windows NT, Windows 98, Windows 2000, Windows XT, Windows XP}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Sample Data Files: DAILY2.DAT; DAILY2.VAR; HOURLY.DAT; HOURLY.VAR; MONTHLYENGY.DAT; }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 MONTHLYENGY.VAR; DAILYTEMPS.DAT; DAILYTEMPS.VAR}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer is a tool for the analysis of building and facility energy use data.  It integrates the previously }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 laborious tasks of data processing, graphing and statistical modeling in a user-friendly, graphical interface.  }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer' easy to use features will help you quickly and accurately determine baseline energy use, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 understand factors that influence energy use, calculate retrofit savings and identify operational and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 maintenance problems.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0  Energy Explorer' powerful data handling features allow you to investigate large data sets with a few simple }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 mouse clicks.  Data can be uploaded directly from Enertel databases or read from ASCII text files.  Time-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 consuming tasks such as data grouping and cleaning, or changing timescales and units can be quickly and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 intuitively accomplished within Energy Explorer and without the need for spreadsheets or specialized }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 programs.  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Data exploration is facilitated by a powerful graphical environment with trend (time series), xy and histogram }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 plots. Individual data points can be identified with click of the mouse.  The user can easily move between }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 long and short-term views by zooming in on areas of interest. Data can even be animated to show the time- }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 sequencing of complex relationships.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer includes with a full package of statistical models specifically designed for building and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 facility energy use.  Models include mean, median, simple and multiple-linear regression.  In addition, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 specially-developed 2, 3, 4 and 5-parameter change-point models allow the user to precisely and easily }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 quantify relationships between building energy use, weather and other energy drivers.  Change-point }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 models accurately model the non-linear energy use patterns characteristic of whole-building electric, steam, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 heat-pump, and cooling energy use data. Modeling results are displayed both as text and graphically to }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 facilitate a quick and complete understanding of the model and its fit to the data.  In addition, retrofit savings }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 and Lean Energy breakdowns can be determined from the regression models in a few simple keystrokes.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0  }{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Getting_Startedx}
${\footnote Getting Started: An Overview of Features}
K{\footnote Getting Started: An Overview of Features}
+{\footnote Browse:0015}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Getting Started: An Overview of Features}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Starting Energy Explorer}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To start Energy Explorer double click on the Energy Explorer icon. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Loading Data from ASCII Files}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer reads ASCII data files that contain only numeric values and have the same number of data }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 elements in each row.  A no-data flag, such as -9.99, should be used in place of unavailable data. The }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 month, day, hour, year and minute (as needed) of each line of data should be listed in separate columns.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer requires that each column of data in the data file be assigned a name.  The easiest way to }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 assign names is to create a short ASCII text file containing the variable names using the Energy Explorer }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 text editor.  To do so, select Edit, Editor from the menu and type each variable name onto a separate line.  }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Save this file in the same directory as the data file and using the same file name as data file, but with the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 filename extension ".VAR". To make full use of Energy Explorer's time series analysis capabilities, use the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 variable names "Mo", "Dy", "Yr", "Hr" and "Mn" to describe data channels containing the month, day, year, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 hour and minute (as needed) of each data record.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To open an ASCII data file, select File, Open Data from Text File from the menu and enter the appropriate }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 path and file name in the File Open dialog box that appears.  Energy Explorer requires that each column of }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 data in the data file be assigned a name.  If Energy Explorer can find the *.VAR variable name file, then it }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 will automatically open it and load the correct variable names.  If not Energy Explorer automatically displays }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the Name Variables dialog box.  Enter descriptive variable names for each column of data in the data file.  }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Save the names as a file in the same directory as the data file and using the same file name as data file, but }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 with the filename extension ".VAR", then click OK.  The variable names will be assigned to the Y Variable }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 and X Variable list boxes.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To merge two data files, such as a file of monthly energy use and a file of average daily temperatures, open }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 a second data file using the same procedure as the first file, then select the Data, Merge With Previous }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Data File menu items. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Creating Time-Series Graphs}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 A time-series graph can be viewed by selecting a variable from the Y Variable box, and then clicking the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Time Series button.  A second time series trace can be displayed on the original time series graph by }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 selecting a second  variable from the Y Variable box and clicking the 2 button adjacent to the Time Series }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 button.  To give each time-series trace the same Y scale, select the menu item Graphics, Single Scale }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 before clicking the 2 button.  To zoom in on a portion of the data, select the menu items Graphics, Zoom In, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 then drag a box over the data of interest using the mouse.  To zoom out, select the menu items Graphics, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Zoom Out.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Creating XY Graphs}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 An XY graph, which shows the relationship between two variables, can be viewed by selecting a Y variable }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 from the Y Variable box, an X variable from the X Variable box, and then clicking the XY button.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Identifying Data Points}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To identify a point on a Time series or XY graph, make sure the menu item Graphics, Point is checked, then }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 simply click on the point using the mouse.  The date and value will be displayed in the Value Bar.  To move }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 to adjacent points, push the right or left arrow keys on the keyboard.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Graph Options}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Grid lines can be displayed and removed by selecting the Graphics, Grid menu items.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Creating Animated XY Graphs}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 An animated XY graph can be viewed by selecting a Y variable from the Y Variable box, an X variable from }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the X Variable box, and then clicking the XY Animate button.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Creating Histogram Graphs}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 A histogram of the data can be viewed by selecting a variable from the Y Variable box and then clicking the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Histogram button.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Grouping and Modifying Data}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Subsets of data can be created by selecting the menu items Data, Select Dates.  To display the dates of the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 current data set select the menu items Data, View Dates.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To modify the values in the data set, select the Data, Modify Variable menu items.  This option can be used }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 to convert units or rescale data.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To divide the data into two groups, such as from the pre and post-retrofit periods, select the menu items }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Data, Group by Date.  The dialog box, enter a Start Date, End Date or both for group 1, then click OK.  To }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 display and model the data as separate groups, click G1G2 at the lower right of the Energy Explorer work }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 window.  To display and model the data as a single group, click the Single Group at the lower right of the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer work window.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Creating Regression Models}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Median, mean, simple linear regression (2-parameter), 3, 4 and 5 parameter change-point and bin-fit }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 models can be activated by selecting Y and X variables from the Y Variable and X Variable box, and then }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 clicking the appropriate model button.  The Median and Mean models only require that a Y variable is }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 selected, while the other models require that both Y and X variables are selected.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 For multivariable linear regression, click the M button next to the 2P model.  For change point + }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 multivariable regression models, click the M next to the appropriate change-point model.  The change-point }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 model will be calculated using X1, the first independent variable selected. In the dialog box that appears, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 select Y from the Regressor box and then your choice of the dependent (Y) variable from the Variable }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Name box.  Click the Set button to lock in the variable.  Then select up to seven independent (X) variables }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 by choosing X1, X2,..., X7 from the Regressor box and selecting a corresponding variable from the Variable }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Name box.  Click the Set button after each choice to lock in the variable. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Prediction intervals  can be displayed and removed by selecting the Graphics, Prediction Intervals options }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 from the menu.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Calculating Savings}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To calculate savings, divide the data into pre and post retrofit periods by selecting the menu item Data, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Group by Date.  Click G1G2 in the lower right of the Energy Explorer work window, then model the pre-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 retrofit data with a median, mean, regression or change-point model.  Select the menu item, Analysis, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Calculate Savings. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Lean Energy Analysis}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To disaggregate energy use into Lean Energy categories of facility, weather-dependent and production-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 dependent use, model energy use using a 3PH-MVR or 3PC-MVR model.  In the dialog box, set Y to the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 energy use, X1 to temperature and X2 to a production-rleated variable.  Select the menu item, Analysis, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Calculate Lean Energy Breakdown. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Printing}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To print the current value window and graph window using the default printer, select File, Print from the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 menu.  To place a copy of the value window or graph window in the clipboard so it can be pasted into other }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Microsoft Windows applications, select Edit, Copy Value Window or Edit, Copy Graph from the menu.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Menu_Bar}
${\footnote Menu Bar}
K{\footnote Menu Bar}
+{\footnote Browse:0030}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Menu Bar}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 The Menu Bar is located at the top of the Energy Explorer work window.  File opening, closing and saving }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 operations, printing, data processing and graphics options are accessed through the menu bar. The menu }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 bar conforms to standard Microsoft Window protocol.  Select menu items by clicking on them with the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 mouse or typing alt + keyletter, where keyletter is the underlined letter in the menu item.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Graph_Window}
${\footnote Graph Window}
K{\footnote Graph Window}
+{\footnote Browse:0035}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Graph Window}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 All graphs are displayed in the Graph Window that lies directly below the Value Window.  This window may }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 be cleared by clicking on the small box in the upper right hand corner of the window.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Variable_List}
${\footnote Variable List Boxes}
K{\footnote Variable List Boxes}
+{\footnote Browse:0040}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Variable List Boxes}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Select variables for viewing or modeling in the Y-Variable and X-Variable list boxes near the right side of the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 work window.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Graph_Buttons}
${\footnote Graph Buttons}
K{\footnote Graph Buttons}
+{\footnote Browse:0045}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Graph Buttons}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Time Series, TimeSeries 2, XY, XY Animate and Histogram graphs are displayed by clicking on these }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 buttons on the right hand side of the Energy Explorer work window.  Graph display the variables selected in  }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the Y Variable and X Variable list boxes.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Model_Buttons}
${\footnote Model Buttons}
K{\footnote Model Buttons}
+{\footnote Browse:0050}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Model Buttons}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Models are activated by clicking on the model buttons located at the bottom of the Energy Explorer work }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 window.  The dependent (Y) variable used for the model is the channel selected in the Y Variable list box }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 and the independent (X) variable is the channel selected in the X Variable list box.  Model parameters are }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 displayed in the Value Window.  For models with zero or one independent variables, values predicted by the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 models are shown as a solid line in the Graph Window.  For multiple independent-variable models, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 predicted values are displayed as heavy boxes.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Grouping_Buttons}
${\footnote Grouping Buttons}
K{\footnote Grouping Buttons}
+{\footnote Browse:0055}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Grouping Buttons}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 The lower right hand corner of the Energy Explorer work window contains two buttons labeled Single Group }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 and G1|G2.  To view and model data without grouping, select the Single Group button.  When the Single }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Group button is selected graphs and models use the complete set of data.  When the G1|G2 button is }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 selected, Energy Explorer produces separate graphs and models for each group of data.}{
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Printing}
${\footnote Printing}
K{\footnote Printing}
+{\footnote Browse:0075}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Printing}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0     To print the Value Window and the Graph Window as they currently appear on the screen  using the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 default printer:}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1.}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Select File, Print from the menu.}{
