\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Figure 3.2.  Data from the sample data file DAILY2.DAT. Each column (field) represents a different data }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 channel. Columns 2, 3 and 4 are month, day and year (the names of all channels are shown in Figure 3.4).  }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Each row (record) represents a day of data.  The -99 values are no-data flags and indicate that no data was }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 available for a particular period.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Format For Date and Time Variables in ASCII Text Data Files}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer can automatically produce time-series graphs, identify the time and date of data points, and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 group data if certain conventions for specifying the names and values of the date and time variables are }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 followed.  These conventions are described in Table 1.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\ul\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 For the field containing:}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\ul\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Use the data format:}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\ul\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Use the variable name}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Year}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1993}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Yr}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Month}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1-12}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Mo}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Day}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1-31}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Dy}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Hour}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 0-23 or 1-24}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Hr}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Minute}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1-60}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Mn}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Day of Week}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1-7: 1=Sun, 2=Mon,...}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 (Any name is acceptable)}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Table 3-1.  Formats and variable names for date and time fields.  These formats must be followed exactly or }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer may not recognize the date-time stamp. (Energy Explorer may be able to read some two-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 digit year dates,  but use of four- digit year dates is strongly encouraged).}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 The date and time fields can appear in any order or location within the data file.  Only the date and time }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 variables that are necessary to identify the date and time need be specified; for example, monthly data }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 requires only the "Yr" and "Mo" variables}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Opening An ASCII Text Data File}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To open a data file for analysis:}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1.}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Select File, Open Data from Text File from the menu.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 2.}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 In the File Open dialog box that appears, select the appropriate path and file name, then press the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 OK button.  The file name, number of records (rows) and fields (columns) in the data file will be displayed in }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the Value Window.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Specifying Variable Names}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 After opening an ASCII data file, Energy Explorer looks for a variable-name file with the same name as the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 data file, but with filename extension ".VAR".  This file should be located in the same path as the data file.  If }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 found, it automatically loads these variable names. To open a different variable-name file, select the menu }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 items: File, Open Names from Text File.  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To create a variable-name file, use the Energy Explorer text editor (or any other text editor or word }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 processor that can save files in ASCII text format).  To do so, select Edit, Editor from the menu and type }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 each variable name onto a separate line.  Save this file in the same directory as the data file using the same }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 file name as data file, but with the filename extension ".VAR". For example the variable-name file for the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 data file "DAILY2.DAT" is "DAILY2.VAR".  To make full use of Energy Explorer' time series capabilities, use }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the variable names "Mo", "Dy", "Yr", "Hr" and "Mn" to describe data channels containing the month, day, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 year, hour and minute (as needed) of each data record.  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 DAILY2.VAR is shown in Figure 3.4.  Since DAILY2.DAT contains data recorded on the daily timescale, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 only the month day and year are needed (hour and minute are unneeded) to specify the date/timestamp of }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 each record.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Figure 3.4.  Variable names in DAILY2.VAR for the for the 9 columns of data in DAILY2.DAT.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Site}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Mo}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Dy}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Yr}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 DOW}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 CW (MBtu/day)}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 HW (MBtu/day)}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 WBE (kWh/day)}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Temp (F)}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 If an appropriate variable name file cannot be found, Energy Explorer displays the Define Variable Names }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 dialog box.  In the Define Variable Names dialog box, the user can accept or change the default variable }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 names and save the new variable names in a separate file for later use. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To change a variable name:}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Click on the variable from the Current Name box that you wish to change.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 2)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Enter a new name in the New Name text box.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 3)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Click the Change button to lock in the new name.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 4)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Repeat this sequence to change other variable names.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 5)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Click the Load Names button to load the new names without saving them, or the Save and Load }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Names button to save the names in a text file, preferably with the same filename as the data file except with }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the extension ".var", before loading them.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To save the current variable names in a separate file:}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 1)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Click the Save and Load Names button.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 2)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Enter a file name, preferably the same filename as the data file except with the extension ".var", in }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the File Save As dialog box that appears. }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 3)}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0\tab}{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Click the OK button on the File Save As dialog box.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0      At any time during the work session, the Define Variables box can be recalled by selecting Data, Name }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Variables from the menu.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Lean_Energy}
${\footnote Lean Energy Analysis}
K{\footnote Lean Energy Analysis}
+{\footnote Browse:0005}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Lean Energy Analysis}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 It is frequently informative to break down total electricity and/or fuel use into three components: weather-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 dependent energy use, production or sales-dependent energy use, and general facility energy use which is }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 independent of weather or production/sales.  In many cases, a high proportion of facility energy use }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 indicates that energy using equipment is not being turned off or controlled to vary with production and/or }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 weather.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To disaggregate energy use into Lean Energy categories of facility, weather-dependent and production-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 dependent use, model energy use using a 3PH-MVR or 3PC-MVR model.  In the dialog box, set Y to the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 energy use, X1 to temperature and X2 to a production-related variable.  Select the menu item, Analysis, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Calculate Lean Energy Breakdown.  Numerical and graphical energy use breakdowns are displayed.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Analysis_Menu}
${\footnote Analysis Menu}
K{\footnote Analysis Menu}
+{\footnote Browse:0020}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Analysis Menu}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer' grouping and modeling capabilities can be used to calculate savings from energy }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 conservation retrofits, disaggregate energy use into Lean Energy components, and perform other analyses.  }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Calculating Savings Overview}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Perhaps the simplest method to measure retrofit energy savings is to compare pre and post-retrofit energy }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 consumption.  This method implicitly assumes that the change in energy consumption between the pre and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 post-retrofit periods is caused solely by the retrofit.  Unfortunately, however, building and plant energy }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 consumption is also influenced by other factors including weather conditions, levels of occupancy, and }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 operating procedures -- all of which may change between the pre and post-retrofit periods.  If these changes }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 are not accounted for, savings determined by this simple method will be erroneous.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Changing weather conditions, in particular, can have a dramatic affect on building energy use and savings }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 determined from measured data.  For example, a simulation study of commercial building energy use in five }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 U.S. cites showed that gas consumption during extreme years varied by about + 25% from average weather }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 years.  Because these deviations are in many cases equal to the magnitude of the expected retrofit savings, }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 the need to account for changing weather when determining savings is clear.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To account for changing weather and operating conditions, a model of pre-retrofit energy use as a function }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 of weather and operating conditions can be generated.  Energy Explorer' regression models have been }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 shown to describe heating and cooling energy use with a Root Mean Square Error of about 10% of the }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 mean energy consumption, when energy use is modeled solely as a function of outside air temperature. }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Model accuracy typically increases when other independent variables that influence energy use are added.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 This pre-retrofit model can then be used to predict the how much energy an un-retrofitted building or plant }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 would consume under post-retrofit conditions.  The predicted energy use can be compared to the measured }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 energy use during the post-retrofit period to determine retrofit savings, even when weather and operating }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 conditions have changed between the pre and post retrofit periods.  Energy Explorer automates this }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 process and makes measuring energy savings more accurate and easier than ever before.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 {\uldb Measuring Retrofit Savings}{\v Hlp_Measuring_Retrofit} }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 {\uldb Savings Measurement Algorithms}{\v Hlp_Savings_Measurement}}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\b\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Lean Energy Analysis Overview}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 It is frequently informative to break down total electricity and/or fuel use into three components: weather-}
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 dependent energy use, production or sales-dependent energy use, and general facility energy use which is }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 independent of weather or production/sales.  In many cases, a high proportion of facility energy use }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 indicates that energy using equipment is not being turned off or controlled to vary with production and/or }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 weather.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 {\uldb Lean Energy Analysis}{\v Hlp_Lean_Energy}}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
#{\footnote Hlp_Energy_Explorer}
${\footnote Energy Explorer Text Editor}
K{\footnote Energy Explorer Text Editor}
+{\footnote Browse:0005}

\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs48\cf1\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer Text Editor}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 Energy Explorer has a text editor to view, write and edit ASCII text files.  Use caution, however, in editing }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 large files since the text editor may take a long time to open large files. To edit very large files, we suggest }
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 using a work processor.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0      }{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0 To open the Energy Explorer text editor, select the menu items File, Editor.  The text editor will appear.}{
\par}\pard
\ql\li0\fi0\ri100{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\ql{\f0\fs24\cf0\up0\dn0
\par}\pard
\page
